    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-2277",
      "knowledge_point_id": "980bb2fb-4bb5-5cbb-aea8-87010493c013",
      "knowledge_point": "Integrating a Sum of Exponential Functions",
      "problem": "$\\int (5{e}^{x}+{2}^{x})\\,\\text{d}x=$",
      "worked_solution": "Here, we combine the sum rule and our exponential rules to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int (5{e}^{x}+{2}^{x})\\,\\text{d}x & =5\\int {e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int {2}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5{e}^{x}+\\frac{{2}^{x}}{ln⁡2}+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
